# The deploy user's ~/.ssh/authorized_keys on the server.
#
# One line, and every option on it is doing work. Copy it, replace the key with
# the public half of the pair whose private half goes into GitHub secrets as
# DEPLOY_SSH_KEY, and put nothing else in this file.
#
#   command="/srv/restyle/deploy.sh"
#       The key runs this and only this. Whatever the client asks to run is
#       discarded and turns up in SSH_ORIGINAL_COMMAND instead, which is where
#       deploy.sh reads the sha from. This is the line that turns "a shell on
#       production, in a web UI" into "a button that ships one named image".
#
#   restrict
#       Turns off port forwarding, agent forwarding, X11, and PTY allocation in
#       one word, and keeps turning off anything added in a later OpenSSH. The
#       forwarding ones matter most: without them the key is a tunnel into
#       whatever the box can reach, which includes Neon.
#
#   from="..."
#       Optional and coarse. GitHub's runners come from a wide, published, and
#       changing set of ranges (https://api.github.com/meta), so this is not a
#       serious control unless you run a self-hosted runner with a fixed
#       address. Leave it off rather than maintain a list you will forget.
#
# The deploy user needs to be in the docker group and to own /srv/restyle. It
# does not need sudo, and should not have it.

command="/srv/restyle/deploy.sh",restrict ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAI_REPLACE_THIS_WITH_YOUR_DEPLOY_PUBLIC_KEY deploy@github-actions
